    "heading": "Starting the Week with a Quick Win",
    "body": "Today's easy puzzle was a nice way to start the morning. I first looked at that bottom corner where the cells had to add up to 10. Since we only had a few dominoes to pick from, like the double six and the five-two, I knew the double six had to be involved somehow. I ended up placing the six-six domino vertically on the right side. That made the math work perfectly with the other pieces nearby because six plus four equals ten, and I had a four available on the three-four domino.\n\nThen I moved to the sum of 4 in the bottom left. I had the zero-four domino and the three-four domino left. By putting the zero next to the four, it cleared up the whole left side. It is always satisfying when the last few pieces just click into place without any second-guessing. The final step was placing the five-two domino at the top, and since five plus nothing is just five, it did not break any of the other rules. Everything just fell right into place!",
  },
  "medium": {
    "heading": "A Middle Ground of Math and Magic",
    "body": "The medium puzzle felt a bit like a balancing act. There were so many regions that needed to sum up to exactly 4! I started by finding the spots that had to be zero. There were two little regions that said the value had to be less than 1, so I knew those were zeros right away at positions 1,3 and 3,4. That gave me a great anchor to work from because those zeros had to be part of my dominoes.\n\nThe trickiest part was the middle where three cells had to add up to 4. I had to experiment with the two-zero and one-zero dominoes to see which way they flipped. Once I realized that one cell was shared between a sum and an equals constraint, the whole middle section opened up. It took a minute of staring at the board, but once I placed the four-two domino at the top, the rest of the sums started making sense. It really is all about finding that one piece that locks the others down.",
  },
  "hard": {
    "heading": "Conquering the Giant Logic Leap",
    "body": "Wow, the hard puzzle today was a real test of patience! Those huge regions where every cell has to be the same value really dominate the board. I spent a long time looking at the two big shapes in the middle. Since they were connected by a single domino, I knew the values of those two regions had to be the numbers on one of my remaining dominoes. It felt like a big chain reaction once I figured that out.\n\nI actually hit a dead end halfway through because I tried to make the largest region all threes. I realized quickly that I did not have enough threes in my pile to fill all those spots! After resetting, I tried using fours instead, which worked much better. The unequal region on the bottom right was the final hurdle, but by then, I only had a couple of dominoes left, so it was just about making them fit the remaining gaps. It was a long climb, but finishing it felt great.",
  },
  "tips": "Try to look for the smallest regions first, especially ones that have a target of zero or one, because they really limit your options. Also, if you see a large region where all the numbers must be equal, count how many of that specific number you have available in your domino set before you commit to it.",
  "learned": "I learned that those big equals regions are actually your best friend, even if they look scary at first. They act like a map for the rest of the board because they force so many other cells to follow a specific pattern. Today also reminded me to slow down and check my sums twice, as one wrong number can mess up the whole grid.",
